From 1d623bb0fb4f9a019ba30e2a147e50e6a9a62a7c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Florian Stosse <1315-Harvester57@users.noreply.git.frama-c.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 18:36:56 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [e-acsl] bittree model: return the block found by pt_lookup

pt_lookup, the exact-address lookup of the Patricia trie behind bt_lookup,
left its result uninitialized when it found the block: the assignment of
the leaf was lost in 29af68df0e ("[eacsl] Add C support for concurrency").
Every operation of the bittree model that looks a block up by its start
address (full_init, mark_readonly, freeable, delete_block, realloc, free)
then read an indeterminate value. With gcc -O2 it behaved as "not found":
\freeable and read-only marks were wrong, blocks were not removed at the
end of their scope, and every free aborted with "Not a start of block".
Without optimisation it was stack garbage, and the program crashed.

Return the leaf when its address is the one looked for, and look up in an
empty trie (all the blocks removed) without dereferencing a NULL root.
bt_clean also asserted that the tree exists, but the tree is only created
when the first block is stored: a program that stores no block (such as
`int main(void)` without --full-mtracking) crashed when it ended.

New test special/e-acsl-bittree-lookup.c, and a run of
special/e-acsl-bittree-model.c without --full-mtracking (dev
configuration).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
